so noguchi memorial institute  it's a biomedical research center in  ghana it's part of the university of  ghana  and one of our key mandates is to  conduct biomedical research to inform  public health policies so  malaria as you know is very important  is a very important public health  concern in sub-saharan africa  and that is my core area of  research so as a team  we approach the pine group  what we normally do is to  look at resistance to malaria parasites  why these malaria parasites become  resistant to the anti-malarial drugs  that  our malaria control programs administer  and we advise these policy makers  on the circulating malaria parasites and  why the drugs are not working and they  need to change  drugs not only do we look at drug  resistance we are also interested in  interventions like vaccines  so if for instance a vaccine is being  introduced  we are interested in looking at the  effect of  these vaccines on the circulating  malaria  population in order to see  why this vaccine is not so efficacious  why is it more efficacious in this  region compared to other regions  and we are also interested in  understanding the mechanisms  underlying either resistance or  the efficacy of a vaccine  and so this is something we've been  doing for a while  now we approached pine group to support  us  to um strengthen our bioinformatics  still with the objective of informing  the public health um policies as we've  been doing  so for drug resistance  we are looking at two drugs  or three drugs the sulfadoxane perimeter  which is a synergistic  drug that is used for  prevention of malaria in pregnant women  and in children living in  seasonal malaria regions  in sub-saharan africa so the idea is to  give it  to these people to prevent malaria or  for the children  so that when the transmission peak is  due  they would have had some um drugs and  it will reduce their um  parasite load so we are interested in  genes that are involved in resistance to  this um drug  with pines we've not just looked at  the mutations in these genes that are  involved in resistance  what we've done is to look at the effect  of these  um circulating mutations or variants  in the ghanaian population and in other  african populations to see  the effect of the variant on the protein  structure  or the active sites of the proteins  to understand why resistance is  ongoing we are also tracking  these mutations in  regions outside ghana other african  countries  to see whether the emerging resistance  patterns that we are observing in ghana  are also being replicated elsewhere  or whether some of the resistant  patterns we are observing in ghana  are just localized  patterns that we are seeing and again  the impact on the proteins themselves  now the second drug that we are  interested in is chloroquine  chloroquine as we know is no longer used  in sub-saharan africa to a larger sense  however we are tracking  the re-expansion  of chloroquine sensitive parasites so  it was topped because the parasites had  developed so much resistance to  this draft that there was no need using  it  but when we left this intervention for a  while  we see that there is the re-expansion of  the  sensitive parasites and this is very  important for us to track  one to see how chloroquine can be reused  either as a single dose for  um prevention of malaria  or in combination with other drugs  now the second reason why we are  interested in chloroquine is that  um some of the currently  being used drugs currently we are using  artemisinin in combination with other  drugs like  amuda queen and  lumifantrine now  the re-imagined  chloroquine sensitive paratha parasites  may also cause um  [Music]  resistance to these partner drugs  so it's important to monitor mutations  in these genes that are involved in  chloroquine resistance  not only to track the re-expansion of  chloroquine-sensitive parasites  but also to see the effect of these  sensitive parasites on the drugs that we  are currently  using and again with pine we are not  stopping  at looking at the existence of these  mutations in  our ghanaian population but we are also  interested in how these mutations are  affecting the active site  of the proteins and  trying to understand exactly the  mechanism of resistance how  resistance actually works because yes  chloroquine  we have some ideas but we still do not  understand exactly how  chloroquine wax and how resistance comes  about  because it is more heterogeneous than  you may think of it so we are trying to  understand  how the variant circulating in the  ghanian population are affecting the  structure of the protein and if we find  that  there are important variants either  acting  in synergy with a known  resistant mutations in order to cause  resistance  then our next step is to see exactly  how this is happening through modeling  or molecular dynamics to understand  exactly the interaction or  the effect of these variants on the  protein  now in terms of  vaccines we are interested in candidates  that are being developed for malaria  vaccine  for the second sporozoid protein of the  malaria parasite  it's the only vaccine  that has been developed to the stage  where it's been  approved and it's currently being  introduced on a pilot  scale in a phase 4 trial in four african  three african countries including ghana  malawi and kenya now  our interest as you may know  is that this  vaccine even though has been approved  was  only about 30 to 50 percent efficacious  in certain groups of children five  months to 17 years old  so we are interested in understanding  why  we are not getting an efficacious  malaria vaccine  yes recently just last month one malaria  vaccine  was tested at the phase two level and we  got about 70  efficacy but what has been  approved which is the rtss vaccine  developed from the circumstance protein  was  only as good as 30 percent so  we are interested in understanding why  the vaccine hasn't been 